  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Spring is the temperate season between winter and summer, characterized by warming temperatures, blooming plants, and longer days. In 2026, the Northern Hemisphere spring officially begins with the vernal equinox on March 20, bringing renewed growth and animal activity. It typically spans March-May (North) or September-November (South). Key Characteristics and Timing Start Date: The 2026 spring equinox occurs on March 20 at 10:46 a.m. EDT, marking the moment the sun crosses the equator. Months: Generally, spring includes March, April, and May in the Northern Hemisphere, while it is September, October, and November in the Southern Hemisphere. Characteristics: This season is marked by rising temperatures, melting snow, and increasing daylight as the hemisphere tilts toward the sun. Meteorological Definition: Meteorologists often define spring as lasting from March 1 to May 31. Effects on Nature Plant Life: Warmer weather and increased sunlight trigger the budding of trees, the germination of seeds, and the blooming of flowers. Animal Life: Animals often emerge from hibernation, migrate back to warmer climates, and begin breeding or nesting activities, with increased food availability. What is the Vernal Equinox?The vernal equinox is the moment when the center of the sun crosses Earth's equator, resulting in nearly equal lengths of day and night across the planet. It is considered the astronomical start of spring in the Northern Hemisphere.",
